.TH SPROUT-MODULES 7 "October 2026" "sprout v0.3.0-wip" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
sprout-modules \- modules available in sprout
.SH MODULES
.TP
.B OS
pretty self explanatory, your OS name & version, detection on most operating systems is done via sysinfo,
however on custom android roms, it is fetched from rom specific build.prop values.
.TP
.B Kernel
fetches the kernel name & version from std::env::consts::OS and the sysinfo crate
.TP
.B Init
fetches the init system of your operating system, for most OSes this is hardcoded, for linux we check
/proc/1/comm, and on android we check for the existance of runsv before deciding wether to display
exclusively "Android Init" or "Android Init + runit"
.TP
.B SDK
android exclusive, checks the SDK version via getprop, warns when SDK/android version are mismatched.
.TP
.B Libc
on most operating systems, this is hardcoded, however on linux its a little different, as theres
2 major libcs.
.TP
.B Shell
shell detection is currently not properly supported on non POSIX or POSIX-like shells.
the shell is fetched via $SHELL, which is unfortunately not always accurate.
.TP
.B Project
fetches basic information about the git repository youre in, currently only parses python and rust
projects.
.TP
.B DE
queries $XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP for your desktop environment, is hidden when DE and WM are the same.
.TP
.B WM
fetching the WM is slightly more complicated, this module is the reason we depend on X11RB, displays
wether your window manager is X11 or Wayland, on non BSD & Linux systems, this is hardcoded.
.TP
.B CPU
utilizes the sysinfo crate to fetch CPU name, core/thread count and checks architecture
.TP
.B Prober
fetches information about the Prober, in this case SPROUT, displaying the binary name, followed by
v{major}.{minor}.{release}-{tag}, note that the tag is optional, and is fetched from compile time
variables or other information
.TP
.B Uptime
fetches uptime and formats it in x days, y hours, z mins, falls back to x seconds if less than a minute
of uptime.
.TP
.B Root Wrapper
detects which root wrapper was used to run SPROUT, for most wrappers it just checks the parent process,
for su and runuser it checks the parent of the parent process (since they spawn a shell).
for pkexec and run0 it detects the environment variables $PKEXEC_UID and $SHELL_PROMPT_PREFIX respectively.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sprout (1)
